d CI width at each k, on the same scale as actual_CIwidth (100x adjExp(k)/CIwidth(k=2)). This is the idealised 1/sqrt(k) narrowing curve - giving the CI width if adding replicates reduced it purely by sampling theory. This is anchored so it equals the observed value (100) at k=2, i.e., it is also 100 at k=2 by construction. ## ratio_diff = multiplicative discrepancy as a percentage: 100 x (ratio−1). +30 means the observed CI is 30% wider than expected at that step; −10 means 10% narrower than expected. This is the quantity the relative band judges (note the band is multiplicative, so the limits are −20% to +25%). Assessed in in_ratio_band. Where ratio = actual_CIwidth/exp_CIwidth (equivalently: CIwidth(k)/adjExp(k)): this is the multiplicative discrepancy between observed and expected (exactly 1 at k=2). ## add_diff = additive discrepancy: actual_CIwidth minus exp_CIwidth, in percentage points of the k=2 CI expected width. This is the vertical distance between the observed and expected curves on the normalised plot; +25 means the observed curve sits 25 points (a quarter of the k=2 width) above expected. Assessed in in_add_band.## informative = whether the position counts towards assessment. TRUE for k≥3; FALSE for k=1 (a single unpooled group; undefined for SYS: var_sys is zero at k=1, so the value shown is the sampling-only width expressed against the systematic k=2 anchor, and carries no systematic-error information) and k=2 (the anchor - both positions forced to 100). ## in_ratio_band = relative criterion: TRUE if ratio lies within [1/tau, tau] = [0.80, 1.25] (observed within ±25% of expected, multiplicatively), FALSE if outside, NA if non-informative. ## in_add_band = absolute criterion: TRUE if |gap_pp| ≤ delta = 25 (observed within 25 percentage points = a quarter of the k=2 expected width), FALSE if outside, NA if non-informative. ##  print(bandlistSYS, row.names = FALSE)## $Tonk##  k actualCI_NoW actualCI_W expCI ratiodiff_NoW ratiodiff_W adddiff_NoW